\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Refusal rate on harmful prompts as a function of abliteration dose $\lambda $ for Gemma-3-12B-IT (blue) and GaMS3-12B-Instruct (amber), in English (solid lines, filled circles; EN-BT arm) and Slovene (dashed lines, hollow squares; item-matched SL-MT arm). $\lambda $ scales each model's own selected abliteration edit (9\nobreakspace  {}steps from 0 to 2; $\lambda =1$ is the selected edit), so equal $\lambda $ does not mean an equal edit across models. Points are J1-judged refusal rates from the Experiment\nobreakspace  {}9 RefusEU-TRAIN probe, with the Hautus correction $(k+0.5)/(n+1)$ used in the Experiment\nobreakspace  {}9 analysis. Shaded bands are Wilson 95\% intervals on the raw proportion. $n=300$ harmful items at $\lambda =0$ and the 100-item core block at every $\lambda >0$. Gemma's Slovene curve lies to the right of its English curve throughout the transition (0.64 vs.\ 0.32 at $\lambda =1$): at each dose, more Slovene refusal remains. GaMS3's Slovene curve lies only slightly above its English curve (largest gap 0.14, at $\lambda =0.5$), and the two meet near the floor from $\lambda =1$ onward. These per-dose gaps are descriptive. The pre-registered cross-model comparison is made at matched English refusal, not at matched dose.}}{8}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dose}{{3}{8}{Refusal rate on harmful prompts as a function of abliteration dose $\lambda $ for Gemma-3-12B-IT (blue) and GaMS3-12B-Instruct (amber), in English (solid lines, filled circles; EN-BT arm) and Slovene (dashed lines, hollow squares; item-matched SL-MT arm). $\lambda $ scales each model's own selected abliteration edit (9~steps from 0 to 2; $\lambda =1$ is the selected edit), so equal $\lambda $ does not mean an equal edit across models. Points are J1-judged refusal rates from the Experiment~9 RefusEU-TRAIN probe, with the Hautus correction $(k+0.5)/(n+1)$ used in the Experiment~9 analysis. Shaded bands are Wilson 95\% intervals on the raw proportion. $n=300$ harmful items at $\lambda =0$ and the 100-item core block at every $\lambda >0$. Gemma's Slovene curve lies to the right of its English curve throughout the transition (0.64 vs.\ 0.32 at $\lambda =1$): at each dose, more Slovene refusal remains. GaMS3's Slovene curve lies only slightly above its English curve (largest gap 0.14, at $\lambda =0.5$), and the two meet near the floor from $\lambda =1$ onward. These per-dose gaps are descriptive. The pre-registered cross-model comparison is made at matched English refusal, not at matched dose}{figure.3}{}}
